    "mcqSelfTest": [
      {
        "mcqId": "M10-MCQ01",
        "stemLatex": "The order and degree of $(1 + (y')^2)^3 = (y'')^2$ are:",
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "A",
            "latex": "$2$ and $2$"
          },
          {
            "id": "B",
            "latex": "$2$ and $3$"
          },
          {
            "id": "C",
            "latex": "$3$ and $2$"
          },
          {
            "id": "D",
            "latex": "$1$ and $2$"
          }
        ],
        "correct": [
          "A"
        ],
        "explanation": "Highest derivative $y''$: order $2$; raise to the $6$th power: $(1+(y')^2)^3 = (y'')^2$ — degree $2$."
      },
      {
        "mcqId": "M10-MCQ02",
        "stemLatex": "The integrating factor of $\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\frac{y}{x} = x^2$ is:",
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "A",
            "latex": "$x$"
          },
          {
            "id": "B",
            "latex": "$e^x$"
          },
          {
            "id": "C",
            "latex": "$\\ln x$"
          },
          {
            "id": "D",
            "latex": "$x^2$"
          }
        ],
        "correct": [
          "A"
        ],
        "explanation": "$P = 1/x$ (after the equation is already normalized): IF $= e^{\\int dx/x} = x$."
      },
      {
        "mcqId": "M10-MCQ03",
        "stemLatex": "The solution of $2xy\\,dx + x^2\\,dy = 0$ is:",
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "A",
            "latex": "$x^2 y = C$"
          },
          {
            "id": "B",
            "latex": "$x y^2 = C$"
          },
          {
            "id": "C",
            "latex": "$x + y = C$"
          },
          {
            "id": "D",
            "latex": "$x^2 + y^2 = C$"
          }
        ],
        "correct": [
          "A"
        ],
        "explanation": "Exact ($M_y = N_x = 2x$); $d(x^2y) = 2xy\\,dx + x^2\\,dy$, so $x^2y = C$."
      },
      {
        "mcqId": "M10-MCQ04",
        "stemLatex": "For $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{x+y}{x}$, the substitution $y = vx$ gives the solution:",
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "A",
            "latex": "$y = x\\ln x + Cx$"
          },
          {
            "id": "B",
            "latex": "$y = x^2 + C$"
          },
          {
            "id": "C",
            "latex": "$y = \\ln x + C$"
          },
          {
            "id": "D",
            "latex": "$y = xe^x + C$"
          }
        ],
        "correct": [
          "A"
        ],
        "explanation": "$v + xv' = 1 + v \\Rightarrow xv' = 1 \\Rightarrow v = \\ln x + C$, i.e. $y = x\\ln x + Cx$."
      },
      {
        "mcqId": "M10-MCQ05",
        "stemLatex": "For $\\frac{dy}{dx} + Py = Qy^3$, the correct substitution is $u$ equals:",
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "A",
            "latex": "$y^2$"
          },
          {
            "id": "B",
            "latex": "$y^{-2}$"
          },
          {
            "id": "C",
            "latex": "$y^3$"
          },
          {
            "id": "D",
            "latex": "$y^{1/3}$"
          }
        ],
        "correct": [
          "B"
        ],
        "explanation": "$u = y^{1-n} = y^{-2}$ converts it to a linear ODE in $u$."
      },
      {
        "mcqId": "M10-MCQ06",
        "stemLatex": "The complementary function of $y'' - 5y' + 6y = 0$ is:",
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "A",
            "latex": "$c_1e^{2x} + c_2e^{3x}$"
          },
          {
            "id": "B",
            "latex": "$(c_1 + c_2x)e^{5x/2}$"
          },
          {
            "id": "C",
            "latex": "$e^{2x}(c_1\\cos x + c_2\\sin x)$"
          },
          {
            "id": "D",
            "latex": "$c_1e^{-2x} + c_2e^{-3x}$"
          }
        ],
        "correct": [
          "A"
        ],
        "explanation": "$m^2 - 5m + 6 = 0 \\Rightarrow m = 2, 3$: distinct real roots."
      },
      {
        "mcqId": "M10-MCQ07",
        "stemLatex": "The complementary function of $y'' - 4y' + 4y = 0$ is:",
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "A",
            "latex": "$(c_1 + c_2x)e^{2x}$"
          },
          {
            "id": "B",
            "latex": "$c_1e^{2x} + c_2e^{-2x}$"
          },
          {
            "id": "C",
            "latex": "$e^{2x}(c_1\\cos 2x + c_2\\sin 2x)$"
          },
          {
            "id": "D",
            "latex": "$c_1e^{2x} + c_2e^{3x}$"
          }
        ],
        "correct": [
          "A"
        ],
        "explanation": "$m^2 - 4m + 4 = 0 \\Rightarrow m = 2$ repeated: $(c_1 + c_2x)e^{2x}$."
      },
      {
        "mcqId": "M10-MCQ08",
        "stemLatex": "A particular integral of $y'' + 4y = \\cos 3x$ is:",
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "A",
            "latex": "$-\\frac{1}{5}\\cos 3x$"
          },
          {
            "id": "B",
            "latex": "$\\frac{1}{5}\\cos 3x$"
          },
          {
            "id": "C",
            "latex": "$\\cos 3x$"
          },
          {
            "id": "D",
            "latex": "$-5\\cos 3x$"
          }
        ],
        "correct": [
          "A"
        ],
        "explanation": "$D^2 \\to -9$: $\\frac{\\cos 3x}{-9 + 4} = -\\frac{\\cos 3x}{5}$."
      },
      {
        "mcqId": "M10-MCQ09",
        "stemLatex": "The degree of $y'' = e^{y'}$ is:",
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "A",
            "latex": "$1$"
          },
          {
            "id": "B",
            "latex": "$2$"
          },
          {
            "id": "C",
            "latex": "undefined"
          },
          {
            "id": "D",
            "latex": "$0$"
          }
        ],
        "correct": [
          "C"
        ],
        "explanation": "A derivative inside $e^{(\\cdot)}$ can never be cleared to a polynomial power."
      },
      {
        "mcqId": "M10-MCQ10",
        "stemLatex": "A particular integral of $y'' + 9y = \\sin 2x$ is:",
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "A",
            "latex": "$\\frac{\\sin 2x}{5}$"
          },
          {
            "id": "B",
            "latex": "$-\\frac{\\sin 2x}{5}$"
          },
          {
            "id": "C",
            "latex": "$5\\sin 2x$"
          },
          {
            "id": "D",
            "latex": "$\\sin 2x$"
          }
        ],
        "correct": [
          "A"
        ],
        "explanation": "$D^2 \\to -4$: $\\frac{\\sin 2x}{-4 + 9} = \\frac{\\sin 2x}{5}$."
      }
    ],
    "memoryHooks": [
      "Order = highest derivative; degree needs a cleared polynomial in $y'$.",
      "Radicals/fractions in derivatives: clear before reading degree.",
      "Derivative inside $\\sin$, $e$, $\\ln$ $\\to$ degree undefined.",
      "Normalize by $a(x)$ FIRST, then IF $= e^{\\int P\\,dx}$.",
      "Exact test: $M_y = N_x$; integrate $M$ w.r.t. $x$.",
      "$y = vx$ for $\\phi(y/x)$; Bernoulli: $u = y^{1-n}$.",
      "Auxiliary roots: distinct $\\to$ two exponentials; repeated $\\to$ $(c_1 + c_2x)e^{mx}$; complex $\\to$ damped oscillation.",
      "$e^{kx}$: $D \\to k$; $\\sin/\\cos$: $D^2 \\to -\\omega^2$ only.",
      "$f(k) = 0$? Multiply by $x$ and differentiate the operator.",
      "Final answer: $y = \\text{CF} + \\text{PI}$."
    ],
    "commonMistakes": [
      "Computing IF before dividing by the $y'$ coefficient.",
      "Reading degree from the un-cleared radical form.",
      "Assigning a degree to $y'' = e^{y'}$-type equations.",
      "Using $u = y^{1-n}$ with the wrong sign in $(1-n)P$.",
      "Replacing $D$ by $-\\omega$ instead of $D^2$ by $-\\omega^2$.",
      "Forgetting the $x$-multiplication rescue when $f(k) = 0$.",
      "Quoting $(c_1 + c_2x)e^{mx}$ for distinct roots.",
      "Missing the $+\\text{PI}$ in the general solution."
    ],
    "examChecklist": [
      "State order and degree after clearing radicals; spot undefined-degree cases.",
      "Normalize, then IF $= e^{\\int P\\,dx}$ for linear ODEs.",
      "Test $M_y = N_x$ before calling an equation exact.",
      "Reduce $\\phi(y/x)$ with $y = vx$.",
      "Bernoulli: divide by $y^n$, substitute $u = y^{1-n}$.",
      "Factor the auxiliary equation and pick the right CF case.",
      "PI: $D \\to k$ for exponentials, $D^2 \\to -\\omega^2$ for trig.",
      "Handle $f(k) = 0$ failures with the $x$-shift rule.",
      "Combine $y = \\text{CF} + \\text{PI}$ at the end."
    ],
